    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the sudden intrusion of the goblin narrative; I must admit, my brain has been so preoccupied with the chaotic energy of a goblin horde raiding a bakery that I forgot to mute the internal monologue for a moment. It is a very strange cognitive dissonance to try to discuss the precise biochemical pathways of lactic acid production while simultaneously imagining goblins wielding pickaxes in the background, but I truly cannot help it.\n\nTo answer your serious question about the microbiology: *Lactobacillus* species lower the pH of the dough by producing lactic acid, which creates an acidic environment. This acidity inhibits the growth of competing spoilage organisms and pathogens, effectively \"sterilizing\" the dough against unwanted invaders. For *Saccharomyces cerevisiae*, this acidic environment is actually beneficial in the long run because it selects for acid-tolerant yeast strains, but it does slow down their initial metabolic rate compared to a neutral pH. The yeast must adapt to this lower pH to continue fermenting sugars into carbon dioxide and ethanol.\n\nHowever, there is a delicate balance here. If the lactic acid production is too rapid or excessive, the pH can drop so low that it eventually",
